# Line endings, scoped deliberately narrowly.
#
# The rule that earns its place is the shell one. tools/ci/preflight.sh and
# tools/ci/protect-main.sh are executed by the Ubuntu runner and by developers in
# Git Bash. A script committed with CRLF from a Windows clone fails as
# `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M`, which reads as a missing binary rather
# than as a line-ending problem, and costs an afternoon to recognise.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the other direction: Windows tooling expects CRLF, which is why
# .pre-commit-config.yaml excludes `\.ps1$` from mixed-line-ending.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# There is deliberately NO global `* text=auto eol=lf` here. 29 tracked files are
# stored CRLF in the index today -- among them apps/api-py/app/config.py and
# app/interview_relay.py, which are live backend source. A global rule would
# renormalise all 29 on the next `git add`, burying a whitespace migration inside
# whatever change happened to carry it. That migration may well be worth doing;
# it is worth doing as its own commit, where the diff is the point rather than
# the noise. Check the current state with `git ls-files --eol | grep i/crlf`.

# Terraform heredocs are sent to AWS verbatim: the CloudFront function body in
# infra/aws/cdn.tf, checked out CRLF on Windows, made `terraform plan` propose
# to rewrite a live function that differed only by a carriage return per line.
# The index already holds the .tf files LF, so this rule renormalises nothing;
# it only stops autocrlf undoing that on checkout (docs/cdk-cutover.md step 0).
*.tf   text eol=lf
